   </text:sequence-decls>
   <text:p text:style-name="P1">Insulated Gate Bipolar Transistors</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P2">James Ryan, Power Electronics, Spring 2026</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P3" text:outline-level="1">Physical Overview</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P4"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T1">Insulated Gate Bipolar Transistors (IGBTs) are a type of transistor which </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T2">can be considered a hybrid between a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3">Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT)</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4"> and a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3">Field Effect Transistor (FET). </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">We will omit a large amount of discussion on every feature of the these devices, and will instead look at their physical operation and how it motivates a need for the IGBT.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P5" text:outline-level="2"><text:span text:style-name="T6">T</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7">he </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">pn </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">junction</text:span></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6">As a reminder, a <text:span text:style-name="T10">pn</text:span> junction is a junction between a <text:span text:style-name="T10">p-type </text:span>and <text:span text:style-name="T10">n-type </text:span>doped semiconductor. <text:span text:style-name="T11">P-</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">type semiconductors have an excess of holes, and </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">n-</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">type semiconductors have an excess of electrons. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">Holes will diffuse to the hole-deficient </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T14">n</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13"> region, and electrons diffuse to the electron-deficient </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T14">p</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13"> region. An electric potential develops, which creates drift forces. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P7"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr1" draw:name="Frame12" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:y="-1.6362in" svg:width="6.9299in" draw:z-index="27">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="2.0299in">
