        </draw:image>
       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure12" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">2</text:sequence>: Potential barrier in a PN Junction, at equilibrium. The drift and diffusion charge carrying currents are equal, and there is no net current. <text:span text:style-name="T15">(Cavallaro)</text:span></text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10">Our <text:span text:style-name="T16">potential barrier</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T17"> forms from these drift and diffusion interactions, allowing no net current through our junction. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T18">Generally, we can overcome this by applying a forward biased potential difference, which will shift the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T19">energy of the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T20">p</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T19"> side closer to the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T20">n</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T19"> side. There is some threshold we can meet where this barrier sufficiently drops, and we can start to conduct again.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr3" draw:name="Frame15" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="5.5799in" draw:z-index="31">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="2.5098in">
