        </draw:image>
       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure14" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">3</text:sequence>: Potential barrier of pn junction at forward bias. Note the difference between the p-type and n-type energy level, indicating to us that work is being done across the barrier! <text:span text:style-name="T13">(Cavallaro)</text:span></text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P12">Applying a positive voltage difference from the <text:span text:style-name="T10">p</text:span> region to the <text:span text:style-name="T10">n</text:span> region will alter the equilibrium, dropping our potential barrier. This is <text:span text:style-name="T10">forward-bias</text:span>. In this state a diffusion current forms, allowing carriers to cross the junction.</text:p>
