        </draw:image>
       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure15" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">4</text:sequence>: <text:s/>Potential barrier of pn junction at forward bias. Note the large gap between energy in p-type and n-type, which opposes the force of diffusion. <text:span text:style-name="T15">(Cavallaro)</text:span></text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P12">Applying a negative voltage difference from <text:span text:style-name="T10">p</text:span> to <text:span text:style-name="T10">n </text:span>will raise our potential barrier, pushing electrons and holes further apart, further opposing diffusion current.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14">So, in an ideal world, this junction lets us conduct current in forward bias, and block it in reverse bias. We’ll see how the BJT (and IGBT) take advantage of these properties to sweep electrons and induce an amplified current flow.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P15" text:outline-level="2">Bipolar Junction Transistors</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16">To review, a <text:span text:style-name="T6">Bipolar Junction Transistor (</text:span>BJT<text:span text:style-name="T6">)</text:span> consists of three <text:span text:style-name="T10">p </text:span>or <text:span text:style-name="T10">n</text:span> doped regions stacked in an alternating style. The outer layers are the collector and emitter, and the center is the base. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:soft-page-break/><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr4" draw:name="Frame1" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="4.8791in" draw:z-index="0">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="2.8244in">
