      <svg:desc>12§display§V_{bc} &lt; 0§svg§600§FALSE§</svg:desc>
     </draw:frame></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T23">)</text:span>, current will flow from the collector to the emitter of a BJT, and this flow is controlled by the base. This happens when the base-emitter region is forward biased and the base-collector region is reversed biased. <text:span text:style-name="T24">In other terms, the voltage at the collector should be higher than the voltage at the base, which should be higher than the voltage at the emitter (relative to ground, assuming threshold voltage is met).</text:span> <text:span text:style-name="T25">When a small current is provided to the base, it flows into the base and out of the emitter. A much larger current is induced from collector to emitter, </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T24">which can be measured by multiplicative factor </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T26">β. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr3" draw:name="Frame2" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="4.2437in" draw:z-index="2">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="2.4764in">
