       <text:p/>
      </draw:image>
      <svg:title>TexMaths</svg:title>
      <svg:desc>12§display§I_e = I_c + I_b§png§600§FALSE§</svg:desc>
     </draw:frame></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T33">). </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">This “sweeping” from base to collector can be summarized in figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure13">8</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">, where our energy levels between the emitter and base allow for conduction of charge carriers, and minus some recombination, our carried charges are swept to the collector region, which strongly induces electrons to flow into its region.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"><text:span text:style-name="T35">As seen in figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T35"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure1">6</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T35">, a typical BJT is physically optimized for active operation in the forward direction, and our emitter region is usually more heavily doped than the collector region. This is done to increase our breakdown voltage and improve our reverse-blocking performance. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T36">Interchanging the collector and emitter pins will usually result in a severely decreased </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T37">β</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T38">.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><text:soft-page-break/><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr3" draw:name="Frame14" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="6.7902in" draw:z-index="35">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="4.6902in">
