        </draw:image>
       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure3" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">9</text:sequence>: (left) I-V Characteristics and (right) ideal operation of a BJT</text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P26">As seen in figure <text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure3">9</text:sequence-ref>, <text:span text:style-name="T41">a </text:span>sufficiently high base current <text:span text:style-name="T41">will turn the device on. Ideally, applying any amount of voltage across the collector to emitter will turn this device on (ideally, a small amount of </text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T41">voltage should do the trick). BJTs take a while to switch: there is a stored charge in the base of the device as a result of the recombination taking place, and when the device leaves saturation mode device has to remove it. Some devices overcome this with more impurity in the base region, to speed up recombination, or with a Baker </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">C</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T41">lamp </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">(see further reading)</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T41">.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P15" text:outline-level="2">Field Effect Transistors </text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P4"><text:span text:style-name="T43">Recall that a capacitor is a two plate device, where, when a voltage difference is applied across it, a charge buildup is formed in between the two plates (</text:span><draw:frame text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:y="-0.1138in" draw:z-index="7" draw:name="Shape2" draw:style-name="gr1" draw:text-style-name="P23" svg:width="0.5996in" svg:height="0.1469in">
