       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure7" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">13</text:sequence>: IGBT Equivalent Circuit <text:span text:style-name="T13">(Mohan)</text:span></text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P35"><text:tab/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P35"><text:span text:style-name="T54">From a high level, our cross section (figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T54"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure6">12</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T54">) is laid similarly to a MOSFET, noting the isolated gate. When we correctly apply a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T55">gate-source voltage</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T54">, a channel forms across the p region (our body region), and “connects” our n+ source region to our drain. However, our drain is not one region, its multiple: a drain drift region (n-), a buffer (n+), and an injecting layer (p+).</text:span></text:p>
