     </draw:frame></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T62">). Removing this layer creates a symmetric (non punch-through) IGBT, and can block both forward and reverse voltages. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T59">Using a punch-through structure is generally preferred, since it provides a lower drift voltage due to the increased resistance it provides, </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T64">and we can easily compensate for the lack of a reverse direction with a freewheeling diode.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P39">It can be said that latch-up is possible in this device. Most holes are attracted to the formed channel, which travel laterally, creating an ohmic voltage drop across the <text:span text:style-name="T10">n+</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="T10">p</text:span> junctions which forward-biases it. If the voltage drop is large enough, a substantial amount of electrons will flow from source to body, and a parasitic <text:span text:style-name="T10">npn </text:span>transistor will turn on within the device, and the gate will no longer be able to control the current flow. <text:span text:style-name="T65">Generally, there is a latching current threshold through the device which the manufacturer will detail in their datasheet; exceeding that current drop within the device can cause a static latch-up. This value is controllable by the manufacturer, mostly by reducing the body spread </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T15">resistance, marked in figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T15"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure8">14</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T15"><text:s/>as the “lateral resistance”.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T66"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T65">For example: keeping the lateral width of the source as slim as possible reduces th</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T15">is</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T65">, and the source regions could have two types of doped regions: a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T66">p+</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T65"> region closest to the metallized source contacts, and then a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T66">p</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T65"> region spanning the channel, reducing lateral resistance. The device could also remove one of the two </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T66">n+ </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T65">source regions, to provide a bypass from lateral current flow, but doing that reduces the device’s transconductance since the width of our gate is reduced.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P34"><text:soft-page-break/><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr3" draw:name="Frame9" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="4.4909in" draw:z-index="22">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="3.8819in">
