        </draw:image>
       </draw:frame>Figure <text:sequence text:ref-name="refFigure9" text:name="Figure" text:formula="ooow:Figure+1" style:num-format="1">15</text:sequence>: (a) IGBT Output I-V Characteristics, (b) Transfer Characteristics, (c) &amp; (d) n-channel IGBT symbols. <text:span text:style-name="T67">For p-channel, reverse the arrow-head directions.</text:span> <text:span text:style-name="T21">(Mohan)</text:span></text:p>
     </draw:text-box>
    </draw:frame></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P40"><text:span text:style-name="T68">Considering the physical construction, we can see that the IGBT can be equivalently modeled as a Darlington pair, with a MOSFET as the driver and a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T69">pnp</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T68"> transistor as our main device. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T59">Generally, our “driver MOSFET” seen in the Darlington equivalent circuit is responsible for most of the driver current.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T68"> Note that our </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">I-V characteristics, diagrammed in figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure9">15</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">(a), are strikingly similar to our BJT’s I-V curve, seen in figure </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42"><text:sequence-ref text:reference-format="value" text:ref-name="refFigure3">9</text:sequence-ref></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T70">Expectedly, because of the gated input, we control the IGBT similarly to controlling a MOSFET, and we get the same benefits: our gate is high impedance, and we only need a small amount of energy to switch the device. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P41">To be explicit: the <text:span text:style-name="T10">pros</text:span> of an IGBT include it’s faster switch speed as compared to a BJT, while maintaining similar on-state losses to a BJT, <text:span text:style-name="T71">making it ideal for high-frequency </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T64">applications which need to operate in a high-current and low saturation voltage scenario. <text:s/>However, we still experience switching loss; the carriers injected into the drain-drift region (our “base”) still take time to discharge when changing state, something which a MOSFET doesn’t experience. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6">When “turning off”, our IGBT’s drain current will rapidly drop, but due to the stored charge in the base, a small current tail forms which takes longer to dissipate. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P42" text:outline-level="1"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T27">Example: Usage in an Induction Heating Circuit</text:span></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P43"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr3" draw:name="Frame3" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="6.9252in" draw:z-index="4">
     <draw:text-box fo:min-height="4.1799in">
